      "raw_response": "It sounds like your team may need a lightweight **work-intake and coordination system** that sits between Slack and the tools where work actually gets tracked.\n\nA strong starting concept:\n\n- **One intake path for requests:** A Slack form, shortcut, or dedicated channel that turns requests into standardized tickets/tasks rather than scattered messages and DMs.\n- **A visible work queue:** A single board or dashboard showing owner, priority, status, due date, and related requests—so people can see whether something is already underway before starting it.\n- **Notification triage:** Rules that separate urgent alerts, actionable team requests, FYI updates, and automated noise. People receive fewer default alerts and can subscribe only to what affects them.\n- **Duplicate detection:** When a new request comes in, it suggests existing tasks, threads, or projects with similar wording before a second effort begins.\n- **Clear ownership and decision rules:** Each item gets one accountable owner, while defined escalation rules prevent everyone from reacting to the same Slack message.\n- **Weekly operational review:** A short recurring review of blocked work, duplicates, old requests, and notification sources that should be muted, consolidated, or automated.\n\nThis could be implemented using your existing stack—often Slack plus a project tracker and automation—or it could justify a custom internal tool if your workflows are unusual.\n\nBefore recommending the exact setup, I’d want to understand:\n\n1. What kind of team is this, and roughly how many people are involved?\n2. Where does work currently get tracked, if anywhere—Slack only, Jira, Asana, Linear, Notion, email, spreadsheets, another system?\n3. What are the main sources of Slack noise: human messages, automated alerts, customer requests, incident notifications, approval requests, or something else?\n4. How does duplicate work usually happen? For example, do multiple people independently inv