      "raw_response": "## My read on it\n\nThose two complaints are usually the same problem wearing two hats: **there's no single place where \"what needs doing\" and \"who has it\" lives**, so the negotiation happens in chat. Every request becomes a message, every message becomes a notification, and because nothing is queryable, two people independently pick up the same thing — or answer the same question for the fifth time.\n\nSo I'd be skeptical of building a \"notification manager.\" Muting noise treats the symptom. The thing worth building is a thin layer that makes work *discoverable before it's started*.\n\n## The shape I'd propose (default option)\n\nA Slack-native intake + dedupe layer over a small work registry:\n\n1. **One intake path.** Requests come in via a Slack shortcut or form in a single channel — not DMs, not five side channels. Output is a record, not a message.\n2. **Dedupe at submit time.** Before the request is created, the bot does a semantic search over open and recently-closed items and shows \"these 3 look similar — join one or continue?\" This is the piece that actually kills duplicate work, and it's cheap to build with embeddings over your existing ticket text.\n3. **Explicit claiming.** One button to take ownership, visible in-channel. Unclaimed items after N hours get surfaced; claimed items stop being re-litigated.\n4. **Batched digests replace realtime pings.** Default is a twice-daily personal digest of what's yours, what's waiting on you, what changed. Only a narrow allowlist (production incidents, direct blockers, explicit @-urgent) pings immediately.\n5. **Answered-question capture.** When a thread resolves, the bot offers a one-click \"save as answer\" so the next identical question hits a searchable answer instead of a human.\n\nRealistically this is 3–6 weeks of work for one competent engineer if you already have a tracker to sit on top of, and most of the value lands in items 2 and 4.\n\n**Two alternatives worth considering instead:** (a) buy it — Linear or Jira plus disciplined Slack integration config gets you maybe 60% of this for near-zero build cost, and the honest answer for a lot of teams is that the tool isn't the problem, the norms are; (b) go smaller — build *only* the dedupe check as a slash command against your existing tracker, ship it in a week, see if duplicate work actually drops before committing to a platform.\n\n## What I need from you before designing anything\n\n**About the duplicate work — this is the most important one.** Give me two or three real recent examples. There's a big difference between: two engineers fixing the same bug, someone rebuilding a report that already exists, the same question being answered repeatedly by different people, or two teams independently starting the same project. Each needs a different mechanism.\n\n**On the team and the work:**\n1. How many people, and is this one team or several that hand off to each other?\n2. Where does work live today — Jira, Linear, Asana, Notion, spreadsheets, nowhere?\n3. Who generates requests? Internal teammates, other departments, or external customers? Roughly how many per week?\n4. Is the work reactive (support, ops, requests coming in) or planned (sprints, roadmap)? Mix?\n\n**On the notification problem:**\n5. Is the pain volume (too many messages) or expectation (people assume instant replies)? Those need different fixes, and the second one is a management problem, not a software one.\n6. How many channels is a typical person in, and are they using @-here liberally?\n7. Is anyone on-call, and does anything genuinely need to interrupt someone within minutes?\n\n**On constraints:**\n8. Do you have engineering capacity to build *and maintain* this, or do you need something you configure rather than run?\n9. What Slack plan, and do you have admin/app-install rights?\n10. Budget shape — is a $10–20/user/month tool acceptable, or is that the reason you're considering building?\n11. Any data sensitivity or self-hosting requirement that rules out third-party tools reading Slack content?\n12. What have you already tried that didn't stick? I'd rather not re-propose it.\n\nAnswer the duplicate-work examples plus 1, 2, 8 and 10 and I can give you a concrete spec — either a build plan or a \"don't build this, do this instead.\"",
