      "raw_response": "# Drug-Drug Interaction Review\n\nThree pairs to evaluate. Two clinically significant interactions identified.\n\n---\n\n## Interaction 1: Warfarin + Ibuprofen — **MAJOR**\n\n**Mechanism (multiple, predominantly pharmacodynamic):**\n1. **Platelet dysfunction:** Ibuprofen reversibly inhibits platelet COX-1, blocking thromboxane A₂ synthesis and impairing platelet aggregation. At 400 mg TID, the antiplatelet effect persists through most of the dosing day. This compromises primary hemostasis while warfarin simultaneously compromises secondary hemostasis (depletion of vitamin K–dependent factors II, VII, IX, X via VKORC1 inhibition) — true pharmacodynamic synergy.\n2. **GI mucosal injury:** Systemic prostaglandin inhibition reduces gastric mucus production, bicarbonate secretion, and mucosal blood flow, promoting erosions and ulceration. This creates a potential bleeding lesion in an already anticoagulated patient.\n3. **Pharmacokinetic contributors (minor / less certain):** Both drugs are ~99% albumin-bound, so transient displacement of warfarin occurs — historically cited, but clinical relevance is limited because free drug is rapidly cleared. Ibuprofen is also a weak CYP2C9 inhibitor and S-warfarin is a CYP2C9 substrate, so modest metabolic inhibition is plausible but not the dominant mechanism. I flag both as secondary and uncertain rather than primary drivers.\n\n**Key clinical point:** Bleeding risk is roughly doubled with this combination (major GI hemorrhage), and — critically — **the risk is not captured by INR monitoring alone**. Patients can bleed at a therapeutic INR because the platelet and mucosal mechanisms are independent of warfarin's anticoagulant intensity.\n\n**Recommended action:**\n- Avoid the combination. Ibuprofen is PRN, so deprescribing is feasible: trial acetaminophen instead (note: acetaminophen ≥2 g/day for several consecutive days can itself elevate INR — mechanism not fully established, possibly NAPQI-mediated interference with the vitamin K cycle — so cap at low doses and monitor INR if used regularly).\n- For localized osteoarthritis (knee/hand), a **topical NSAID** (e.g., topical diclofenac) is an excellent alternative — systemic absorption is minimal and interaction risk is negligible.\n- If oral NSAID use is truly unavoidable: lowest effective dose, shortest duration, add a PPI for gastroprotection, recheck INR within 3–7 days of initiation or discontinuation, and counsel explicitly on melena, hematemesis, and unusual bruising. Verify no additional OTC NSAID use.\n\n---\n\n## Interaction 2: Warfarin + Lisinopril — **No clinically significant interaction**\n\n**Mechanism assessment:** Lisinopril is not a CYP450 substrate (excreted renally as unchanged drug), does not affect vitamin K epoxide reductase or clotting factor synthesis, and does not meaningfully displace warfarin from albumin. I am not aware of any documented pharmacokinetic or pharmacodynamic interaction between these agents, and major interaction databases list none. I state this with high confidence.\n\n**Recommended action:** None. Continue both as prescribed.\n\n---\n\n## Interaction 3: Ibuprofen + Lisinopril — **MODERATE**\n\n**Mechanism (pharmacodynamic, all renal):**\n1. **Hemodynamic antagonism at the glomerulus:** Lisinopril dilates the efferent arteriole (via reduced angiotensin II), lowering intraglomerular pressure. Ibuprofen constricts the afferent arteriole (via inhibition of vasodilatory prostaglandins PGE₂/PGI₂). The combination reduces renal perfusion from both sides → risk of prerenal acute kidney injury, particularly in older patients, volume depletion, CKD, or heart failure. If a diuretic is ever added, this becomes the classic \"triple whammy\" with substantially higher AKI risk.\n2. **Blunted antihypertensive effect:** NSAID-induced sodium/water retention and loss of prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation antagonize ACE inhibitor efficacy (modest average BP elevation, but variable between patients).\n3. **Additive hyperkalemia risk:** ACE inhibitors reduce aldosterone-mediated K⁺ secretion; NSAIDs suppress renin release (hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism) and reduce distal sodium delivery. Both mechanisms impair potassium excretion.\n\n**Recommended action:**\n- Baseline and follow-up serum creatinine/BUN and potassium, especially after any escalation from PRN to scheduled NSAID use; recheck blood pressure.\n- Lowest effective dose, shortest duration; PRN use (as currently prescribed) is meaningfully lower risk than scheduled use.\n- Counsel on maintaining hydration and holding the NSAID during acute illness with poor intake or GI losses.\n- The same recommendation from Interaction 1 applies — switching analgesia resolves this interaction as well.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Pair | Severity | Primary mechanism |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warfarin + Ibuprofen | Major | Pharmacodynamic synergy: COX-1 platelet inhibition + GI mucosal injury (± minor PK effects) |\n| Warfarin + Lisinopril | None | — |\n| Ibuprofen + Lisinopril | Moderate | Pharmacodynamic antagonism: renal hemodynamics, BP blunting, hyperkalemia |\n\n**Overall clinical takeaway:** One intervention — replacing scheduled oral ibuprofen with acetaminophen or a topical NSAID — eliminates both identified interactions and is the highest-yield change to this regimen.",
