      "raw_response": "# Contract Review Assistant — 7-Day MVP Build Plan\n\n**Team:** 3 engineers (Eng A: backend/AI pipeline, Eng B: frontend, Eng C: infra + full-stack glue)\n**Stack summary:** Next.js 14 + TypeScript frontend, Python FastAPI backend, PostgreSQL, Celery + Redis workers, OpenAI GPT-4o, Clerk auth, S3 storage, Vercel + Render hosting.\n\n---\n\n## 1. System Architecture\n\n**Components and data flow:**\n\n```\n[Browser: Next.js] --HTTPS--> [FastAPI API] --> [PostgreSQL]\n                      |             |\n                      |             +--> [Clerk JWKS] (JWT verify)\n                      |             |\n                      +--direct PUT--> [S3] (presigned upload)\n                                      |\n                            [Celery Worker] <--> [Redis broker]\n                                  |\n                                  +--> [OpenAI API] (extraction/risk/redline)\n                                  +--> [S3] (fetch source doc)\n```\n\n**Flow:**\n1. Client requests a presigned S3 URL, uploads the PDF/DOCX directly to S3 (keeps large files off the API server).\n2. Client calls `POST /v1/documents` with the S3 key. API creates a `documents` row (`status=queued`) and a `jobs` row, enqueues a Celery task.\n3. Worker pipeline (state machine per document): `queued → extracting → segmenting → analyzing → redlining → reporting → ready` (or `failed`).\n4. Worker writes pages, clauses, findings, redlines, and report to Postgres as each stage completes (partial results visible early).\n5. Frontend polls `GET /v1/documents/{id}/status` every 3s while processing, then fetches full results.\n6. Every LLM call is logged (model, tokens, latency, cost, prompt version) to `llm_calls` for cost tracking and debugging.\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n- Async everything: no LLM work in the request path. Max request latency stays <300ms.\n- Org-scoped multi-tenancy: every table carries `org_id`; every query filters on it.\n- Pipeline stages are idempotent Celery tasks keyed by `document_id`, so retries are safe and re-analysis is just re-enqueueing from a stage.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Database Schema (PostgreSQL 15, SQLAlchemy 2.0 + Alembic)\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE organizations (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  clerk_org_id TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  risk_profile JSONB NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',   -- per-org severity thresholds\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  clerk_user_id TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  org_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES organizations(id),\n  email TEXT NOT NULL,\n  role TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (role IN ('admin','member','viewer')),\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  org_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES organizations(id),\n  uploaded_by UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES users(id),\n  filename TEXT NOT NULL,\n  s3_key TEXT NOT NULL,\n  mime_type TEXT NOT NULL,\n  page_count INT,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'queued'\n    CHECK (status IN ('queued','extracting','segmenting','analyzing',\n                      'redlining','reporting','ready','failed')),\n  error_code TEXT,\n  risk_score NUMERIC(5,2),                    -- 0-100 aggregate\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_documents_org ON documents(org_id, created_at DESC);\n\nCREATE TABLE document_pages (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  page_number INT NOT NULL,\n  text TEXT NOT NULL,\n  UNIQUE(document_id, page_number)\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE clauses (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  order_index INT NOT NULL,\n  clause_type TEXT NOT NULL,                  -- e.g. 'indemnification'\n  title TEXT,                                 -- e.g. 'Section 8.2 Indemnification'\n  text TEXT NOT NULL,\n  page_start INT, page_end INT,\n  char_start INT, char_end INT,               -- anchor into full text\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\nCREATE INDEX idx_clauses_doc ON clauses(document_id, order_index);\n\nCREATE TABLE risk_findings (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  clause_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES clauses(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  category TEXT NOT NULL,                     -- 'unlimited_liability', etc.\n  severity TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (severity IN ('critical','high','medium','low','info')),\n  rationale TEXT NOT NULL,\n  verbatim_quote TEXT NOT NULL,               -- validated substring of clause text\n  confidence NUMERIC(3,2) NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'open'\n    CHECK (status IN ('open','accepted','dismissed')),\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE redline_suggestions (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  finding_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES risk_findings(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  original_text TEXT NOT NULL,\n  suggested_text TEXT NOT NULL,\n  explanation TEXT NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'pending'\n    CHECK (status IN ('pending','accepted','rejected','edited')),\n  edited_text TEXT,                           -- lawyer's modified version\n  decided_by UUID REFERENCES users(id),\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE reports (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  summary JSONB NOT NULL,                     -- exec summary, top risks, stats\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE jobs (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  stage TEXT NOT NULL,\n  status TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('pending','running','succeeded','failed')),\n  attempt INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  error TEXT,\n  started_at TIMESTAMPTZ, finished_at TIMESTAMPTZ\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE llm_calls (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  document_id UUID REFERENCES documents(id) ON DELETE SET NULL,\n  stage TEXT NOT NULL,                        -- 'segment','classify','risk','redline','summary'\n  model TEXT NOT NULL,\n  prompt_version TEXT NOT NULL,               -- e.g. 'risk_v3'\n  input_tokens INT NOT NULL, output_tokens INT NOT NULL,\n  latency_ms INT NOT NULL,\n  cost_usd NUMERIC(8,5) NOT NULL,\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n\nCREATE TABLE audit_events (\n  id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  org_id UUID NOT NULL,\n  user_id UUID,\n  action TEXT NOT NULL,                       -- 'document.upload','redline.accept',...\n  entity_type TEXT, entity_id UUID,\n  metadata JSONB,\n  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now()\n);\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Backend API (FastAPI, prefix `/v1`)\n\nAll endpoints require `Authorization: Bearer <clerk_jwt>`. All list endpoints support `?limit=&cursor=`.\n\n**`POST /v1/uploads/presign`** — get S3 upload URL\n```json\n// Request\n{ \"filename\": \"msa-acme.pdf\", \"mime_type\": \"application/pdf\", \"size_bytes\": 2400000 }\n// Response 200\n{ \"upload_url\": \"https://s3.../signed\", \"s3_key\": \"org_123/2026/07/abc123-msa-acme.pdf\", \"expires_in\": 900 }\n```\n\n**`POST /v1/documents`** — register uploaded doc, start pipeline\n```json\n// Request\n{ \"s3_key\": \"org_123/.../msa-acme.pdf\", \"filename\": \"msa-acme.pdf\" }\n// Response 201\n{ \"id\": \"doc_9f2...\", \"status\": \"queued\", \"created_at\": \"2026-07-24T10:00:00Z\" }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/documents`** — list\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"items\": [\n  { \"id\": \"doc_9f2\", \"filename\": \"msa-acme.pdf\", \"status\": \"ready\",\n    \"risk_score\": 72.5, \"page_count\": 34, \"uploaded_by\": \"jane@acme.com\",\n    \"created_at\": \"...\" }\n], \"next_cursor\": null }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/documents/{id}/status`** — lightweight poll\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"id\": \"doc_9f2\", \"status\": \"analyzing\", \"progress\": {\n    \"pages_extracted\": 34, \"clauses_found\": 28,\n    \"clauses_analyzed\": 17, \"clauses_total\": 28 } }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/documents/{id}/clauses`**\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"items\": [\n  { \"id\": \"cl_01\", \"order_index\": 8, \"clause_type\": \"limitation_of_liability\",\n    \"title\": \"Section 9. Limitation of Liability\", \"page_start\": 14, \"page_end\": 15,\n    \"text\": \"IN NO EVENT SHALL EITHER PARTY'S LIABILITY...\",\n    \"finding_count\": 2, \"max_severity\": \"high\" } ] }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/documents/{id}/findings?severity=high,critical`**\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"items\": [\n  { \"id\": \"rf_77\", \"clause_id\": \"cl_01\", \"clause_title\": \"Section 9...\",\n    \"category\": \"uncapped_liability\", \"severity\": \"high\", \"confidence\": 0.91,\n    \"rationale\": \"Liability cap is absent; indemnification carve-outs expose you to unlimited consequential damages.\",\n    \"verbatim_quote\": \"IN NO EVENT SHALL EITHER PARTY'S LIABILITY...\",\n    \"status\": \"open\" } ] }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/findings/{id}`** — finding detail incl. redline\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"id\": \"rf_77\", \"...\": \"...\", \"redline\": {\n    \"id\": \"rl_12\", \"original_text\": \"IN NO EVENT SHALL EITHER PARTY'S LIABILITY EXCEED...\",\n    \"suggested_text\": \"IN NO EVENT SHALL EITHER PARTY'S LIABILITY EXCEED THE FEES PAID IN THE PRIOR 12 MONTHS...\",\n    \"explanation\": \"Adds a mutual 12-month fees cap, market standard for MSAs.\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\" } }\n```\n\n**`POST /v1/findings/{id}/status`** — `{ \"status\": \"dismissed\" }` → `200 { \"id\": \"rf_77\", \"status\": \"dismissed\" }`\n\n**`POST /v1/redlines/{id}/decision`**\n```json\n// Request\n{ \"decision\": \"edited\", \"edited_text\": \"IN NO EVENT... EXCEED 2X THE FEES PAID...\" }\n// Response 200\n{ \"id\": \"rl_12\", \"status\": \"edited\" }\n```\n\n**`GET /v1/documents/{id}/report`**\n```json\n// Response 200\n{ \"executive_summary\": \"This MSA contains 3 high-severity issues...\",\n  \"risk_score\": 72.5,\n  \"stats\": { \"critical\": 1, \"high\": 2, \"medium\": 5, \"low\": 4 },\n  \"top_issues\": [ { \"finding_id\": \"rf_77\", \"title\": \"Uncapped liability\", \"severity\": \"high\" } ],\n  \"clause_coverage\": [ { \"type\": \"termination\", \"present\": true } ] }\n```\n\n**`POST /v1/documents/{id}/report/export`** → `{ \"format\": \"pdf\" }` → `202 { \"download_url\": \"<presigned>\" }`\n\n**`POST /v1/documents/{id}/reanalyze`** — re-run from `segmenting` stage. Returns `202`.\n\n**`POST /webhooks/clerk`** — sync user/org create/update/delete.\n\n**Errors:** consistent envelope `{ \"error\": { \"code\": \"DOCUMENT_TOO_LARGE\", \"message\": \"...\", \"request_id\": \"req_...\" } }` with proper status codes (400/401/403/404/409/422/429/500).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Authentication & Authorization\n\n- **AuthN: Clerk.** Hosted login/signup (email + Google OAuth), Clerk Organizations for B2B tenancy. Frontend uses `@clerk/nextjs`; session JWTs are short-lived.\n- **Backend verification:** FastAPI dependency verifies the JWT against Clerk's JWKS endpoint (cached public keys), extracts `sub` (user) and `org_id`, loads the internal `users` row. Reject with 401 otherwise.\n- **AuthZ (RBAC):** three roles stored on `users`:\n  1. `admin` — manage members, set risk profile, delete documents, everything below.\n  2. `member` — upload, analyze, accept/dismiss findings and redlines, export.\n  3. `viewer` — read-only.\n- Enforced via a FastAPI dependency (`require_role(\"member\")`) plus **mandatory `org_id` filter on every query** — a single shared SQLAlchemy session factory injects `org_id` from the request context so cross-tenant reads are structurally impossible.\n- **Webhooks:** Clerk webhook (verified with signing secret) provisions/updates `organizations` and `users`.\n- **Security basics:** presigned S3 URLs scoped to the org's prefix with 15-min expiry; S3 bucket private with SSE-KMS; all traffic TLS; `audit_events` for every mutating action; OpenAI API used with zero data retention (no training on customer data); document hard-delete endpoint for compliance requests.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Frontend — Key Screens & Navigation\n\n**Framework:** Next.js 14 (App Router) + TypeScript + Tailwind + shadcn/ui + TanStack Query + Zustand (local UI state) + `react-dropzone` + `react-diff-viewer-continued`.\n\n**Navigation:** left sidebar — *Dashboard | Upload | Documents | Settings*. Top bar — org switcher (Clerk), user menu.\n\n1. **Login/Signup** — Clerk-hosted components, org creation on first signup.\n2. **Dashboard** — stat cards (docs this month, avg risk score, open high-severity findings), recent documents table with status chips (`Processing…` with live progress bar / `Ready` / `Failed` + retry).\n3. **Upload** — drag-drop multi-file zone, per-file progress, \"Analyze\" CTA; client-side validation (type, 25MB / 100-page soft cap warning).\n4. **Document Review Workspace** (the core screen, `/documents/[id]`) — three panes:\n   - Left: clause outline (clickable, color-coded severity dots).\n   - Center: document text viewer with highlighted finding spans; clicking a highlight opens its finding.\n   - Right: finding detail panel — severity badge, rationale, verbatim quote, and the **redline diff view** (original vs. suggested) with Accept / Reject / Edit actions.\n   - Toolbar: severity filter, risk score badge, \"Export Report\" button.\n5. **Report View** (`/documents/[id]/report`) — executive summary, severity breakdown chart, top issues table linking back to findings, clause coverage matrix; Export as PDF/DOCX.\n6. **Settings** — members & roles (Clerk OrganizationSwitcher/invites), risk profile (severity threshold sliders per clause category — simple form over the `risk_profile` JSONB), danger zone (delete org data).\n\nEmpty states, skeleton loaders, and optimistic updates on accept/reject are required polish, not optional — lawyers will judge the tool on feel.\n\n---\n\n## 6. AI/LLM Pipeline\n\n**Models:** GPT-4o-mini for extraction/classification (cheap, high volume); GPT-4o for risk analysis, redlines, and summaries (quality matters). All calls use **OpenAI Structured Outputs** (`response_format: json_schema`, `strict: true`), temperature 0, `tenacity` retries (3 attempts, exponential backoff), and an `asyncio` semaphore (10 concurrent calls). Prompt templates are versioned files (`risk_v3.md`) with the version logged per call.\n\n**Stage 0 — Text extraction.** PDF: `pdfplumber` (with `PyMuPDF` fallback). DOCX: `python-docx`. Preserve a page map (char offset → page). Detect scanned PDFs (<20 chars/page average) → fail fast with `error_code=SCANNED_PDF_UNSUPPORTED` and a clear UI message.\n\n**Stage 1 — Clause segmentation.** Heuristic pre-split on numbered headings (regex for `^\\d+(\\.\\d+)*\\.?\\s+[A-Z]`, ALL-CAPS lines), then GPT-4o-mini confirms boundaries and titles per chunk:\n```json\n{ \"clauses\": [ { \"title\": \"9. Limitation of Liability\", \"start_char\": 12410, \"end_char\": 14022 } ] }\n```\nValidate: spans must be non-overlapping, ordered, and in-bounds; else fall back to heuristic-only segmentation.\n\n**Stage 2 — Clause classification.** Batch 8 clauses per call to GPT-4o-mini; each clause classified into a fixed taxonomy (~20 types: indemnification, limitation_of_liability, termination, payment_terms, ip_ownership, confidentiality, non_compete, non_solicit, governing_law, auto_renewal, warranty, sla, data_protection, assignment, force_majeure, insurance, audit_rights, exclusivity, escalation, other). Output includes `present_but_unusual` flags.\n\n**Stage 3 — Risk analysis.** For each clause (batched 4/call), GPT-4o receives: clause text, clause type, and the org's **risk playbook** (MVP: a versioned JSON of ~60 rules, e.g. `\"liability_cap_missing\" → severity high; \"termination_for_convenience < 30 days notice\" → medium`; org overrides from `risk_profile`). Output:\n```json\n{ \"findings\": [ { \"category\": \"uncapped_liability\", \"severity\": \"high\",\n    \"verbatim_quote\": \"<exact substring of the clause>\",\n    \"rationale\": \"...\", \"confidence\": 0.91 } ] }\n```\n**Anti-hallucination gate:** every `verbatim_quote` must fuzzy-match (≥95% similarity, normalized whitespace) the source clause text, and the category must exist in the playbook — otherwise the finding is dropped and logged. This single check eliminates the most dangerous failure mode (invented clauses).\n\n**Stage 4 — Redline generation.** For findings of severity ≥ medium (capped at 15/doc for cost), GPT-4o generates `suggested_text` constrained by instructions: preserve defined terms exactly, keep formatting style, change only what's needed to resolve the finding, output plain replacement language plus a one-paragraph explanation written for the counterparty.\n\n**Stage 5 — Summary report.** One GPT-4o call over the aggregated findings JSON (not the full contract) producing the executive summary, top-5 issues, and clause coverage. Risk score = deterministic weighted sum (critical=25, high=12, medium=5, low=1, capped at 100), computed in code — never trust the LLM with arithmetic.\n\n**Quality harness:** a golden set of 10 real contracts (MSAs, NDAs, DPAs, SOWs) with hand-labeled expected findings; a `make eval` script runs the pipeline and reports precision/recall of findings and citation-validity rate. Run from Day 3 onward, target ≥90% citation validity before launch.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Deployment Stack & Infrastructure\n\n- **Frontend:** Vercel (preview deployments per PR).\n- **Backend:** Render — one Web Service (FastAPI, gunicorn/uvicorn, autoscaling min 1) + one Background Worker (Celery). Deploy via `render.yaml` (infra-as-code without Terraform overhead).\n- **Database:** Neon Postgres (serverless, branching for dev).\n- **Queue/broker:** Upstash Redis (also caches status polls).\n- **Storage:** AWS S3 (private bucket, KMS encryption, lifecycle rule: raw uploads → Glacier after 90 days).\n- **Auth:** Clerk.\n- **CI/CD:** GitHub Actions — ruff + mypy + pytest (backend), eslint + tsc + vitest (frontend), auto-deploy `main` → production after green checks; database migrations via Alembic run as a Render pre-deploy step.\n- **Secrets:** Render env groups + Clerk/OpenAI dashboards; never in repo. Environments: `dev` (Neon branch), `staging`, `prod`.\n- **Post-MVP migration path:** this maps 1:1 onto AWS (ECS Fargate + RDS + ElastiCache + SQS) when scale demands it — no architectural changes needed.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Observability, Logging & Error Handling\n\n- **Error tracking:** Sentry on both ends (`sentry-sdk` FastAPI + Celery integrations, `@sentry/nextjs`), release-tagged, alerts to Slack on new issues and error-rate spikes.\n- **Structured logs:** `structlog` JSON everywhere; every log line carries `request_id`, `org_id`, `document_id`, `stage`. Render drains to Better Stack for search/retention.\n- **LLM tracing:** every call written to `llm_calls` (tokens, latency, cost, prompt_version) + Helicone proxy for full prompt/response inspection during development (disabled in prod for data-minimization, or enabled per-org with consent).\n- **Metrics:** pipeline stage durations, queue depth, findings/doc, citation-validation failure rate, cost/doc — exposed via a `/internal/metrics` endpoint and a tiny admin page; alert if queue depth > 50 for 10 min or citation-validity drops below 85%.\n- **Uptime:** Better Stack monitor on `/v1/health` (checks DB + Redis connectivity), 30s interval, Slack/SMS alerts.\n- **Error handling doctrine:**\n  - Celery: 3 retries with exponential backoff per stage; on final failure set `documents.status=failed` + machine-readable `error_code` (`SCANNED_PDF_UNSUPPORTED`, `LLM_TIMEOUT`, `DOC_TOO_LARGE`…) that maps to a specific, human UI message with a retry