idate; afternoon bug bash.**\n**Day 7 — Polish & launch.** Freeze at noon. Run 20 fresh contracts through, fix P0s only, parallelization/cost tuning pass, verify `llm_calls` cost reporting, production deploy, smoke test, write runbook (how to retry a stuck doc, rotate keys, read dashboards), record demo. **Deliverable: production MVP + runbook + demo video.**\n\nExplicit buffer: Day 7 afternoon is slack. If the pipeline slips, cut in this order: DOCX export → clause coverage matrix → risk profile UI (fall back to default playbook).\n\n---\n\n## 10. Cost Estimate — 100 Active Users/Month\n\n**Assumptions:** each active user reviews ~10 contracts/month → **1,000 contracts/month**, avg 30 pages (~21k tokens). Per contract, across all stages: ~100k input + ~20k output tokens, split ~60% GPT-4o-mini / 40% GPT-4o. Reanalysis adds ~30% overhead.\n\n**AI usage:**\n| Stage | Model | Cost/contract |\n|---|---|---|\n| Segmentation + classification | GPT-4o-mini ($0.15/$0.60 per 1M tok) | ~$0.02 |\n| Risk analysis | GPT-4o ($2.50/$10 per 1M tok) | ~$0.13 |\n| Redlines (~10/doc) | GPT-4o | ~$0.10 |\n| Summary report | GPT-4o | ~$0.05 |\n| **Subtotal** | | **~$0.30/contract** |\n\n→ 1,000 contracts × $0.30 × 1.3 (reanalysis) ≈ **$400/month AI spend** (~$0.40/contract).\n\n**Infrastructure:**\n| Service | Monthly |\n|---|---|\n| Vercel Pro | $20 |\n| Render web + worker (Standard) | $75 |\n| Neon Postgres (Launch) | $19 |\n| Upstash Redis | $10 |\n| AWS S3 + KMS + data transfer | $15 |\n| Clerk Pro (10k MAU included) | $25 |\n| Sentry Team | $26 |\n| Better Stack + PostHog (free tiers) | $0 |\n| Domain, misc | $10 |\n| **Infra subtotal** | **~$200** |\n\n**Total: ~$600/month** (~$6/active user/month, ~$0.60/contract all-in). At a plausible B2B price of $99–299/user/month, gross margin >90%. Helicone free tier covers LLM tracing during MVP.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Top 5 Technical Risks & Mitigations\n\n1. **LLM hallucinated clauses or citations** — the trust-killer for legal users. *Mitigations:* strict JSON-schema outputs; mandatory verbatim-quote fuzzy-match validation against source text (drop invalid findings, log rate); playbook-constrained categories; every finding UI element deep-links to the highlighted source span; golden-set eval gates release (≥90% citation validity).\n2. **Scanned/image PDFs with no text layer** — common for signed contracts. *Mitigations:* detect at extraction (<20 chars/page) and fail with a precise, actionable message; track occurrence rate; if >10% of uploads, integrate AWS Textract as an OCR fallback (pre-scoped, ~1 day of work, kept out of the critical path).\n3. **Anchoring LLM output back to exact document locations** — offsets drift, quotes mismatch, highlights land wrong. *Mitigations:* pipeline works on char spans from the extractor, never LLM-invented positions; quotes validated as substrings; the MVP shows redlines as clause-level cards rather than fragile inline editing of the original document.\n4. **Latency and cost blowups on large contracts** — a 200-page agreement could take 10+ minutes and $5+ serially. *Mitigations:* 100-page / 25MB cap with clear messaging; full async pipeline with progress UI; batching (8 clauses/classify call, 4/risk call) + 10-way call concurrency; model tiering (mini for cheap stages); cap redlines at 15/doc; per-org rate limits; per-doc cost ceiling that short-circuits to partial results.\n5. **Prompt injection and confidentiality** — contract text could contain adversarial instructions (\"ignore your guidelines\"), and legal teams will not adopt a tool that leaks data. *Mitigations:* contract text always wrapped in delimiters and treated as untrusted data; system prompt explicitly forbids following instructions found in documents; output validated structurally so injected content can't alter schema; zero-retention OpenAI API terms; org-level data isolation enforced in the DB layer; KMS encryption at rest; hard-delete endpoint; written data-handling summary for prospects.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Explicitly Out of MVP Scope\n\n1. **True Word track-changes output** — redlines live as an in-app diff + exported report; native `.docx` with `w:ins`/`w:del` revision markup is post-MVP.\n2. **OCR for scanned PDFs** — detected and messaged; Textract integration is the fast-follow.\n3. **Chat-with-contract / freeform Q&A** — structured findings only, no conversational interface.\n4. **Custom playbook builder UI** — orgs get threshold sliders over the default playbook; full rule authoring is post-MVP.\n5. **Third-party integrations** — no Salesforce, DocuSign, Ironclad, iManage, SharePoint, or Slack integrations.\n6. **Real-time collaboration** — no multi-cursor editing, comments, or @mentions; audit log captures who did what, asynchronously.\n7. **Version-to-version comparison** — comparing two drafts of the same contract is out.\n8. **Enterprise identity** — no SAML SSO or SCIM (Clerk orgs + Google OAuth only).\n9. **Non-English contracts** — English only; other languages rejected with a clear message.\n10. **E-signature, approval workflows, CLM lifecycle, obligation tracking, renewal reminders** — this is a review tool, not a contract management system.\n11. **Fine-tuning and cross-contract RAG** — no customer-data training, no benchmark clause library; prompt engineering only.\n\nThe deliberate trade: depth on the single workflow *upload → trustworthy findings → actionable redlines → exportable report*, at the expense of breadth. That's what gets a legal team to pay after a pilot.",
